{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310536,"nonce":"3z+jwmyci6Tghz3R5zwDGQtXn3U=","title":"TierPreset::Benchmark had blazing_threshold u64::MAX, so rayzor-tiered could never reach the LLVM tier once the runner's manual upgrade was ...","message":"`TierPreset::Benchmark` shipped `blazing_threshold: u64::MAX` with the comment\n\"Manual LLVM upgrade after warmup\". The benchmark runner compensated by calling\n`upgrade_to_llvm()` by hand on the tiered arm. Removing that call (2026-08-23,\nat the user's instruction — an explicit upgrade defeats the point of measuring a\ntiered runtime) left **nothing** to reach the top tier: `rayzor-tiered` was\npermanently capped below LLVM.\n\nMeasured on the NUC, HEAD 6710bb80 vs the same box with the change:\n\n| bench | tiered | cranelift | llvm |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| nbody | 413.26 ms | 394.49 ms | 348.65 ms |\n| binarytrees | 8459 ms | 647 ms | 628 ms |\n\nnbody tiered sat exactly on Cranelift, which is what \"nbody is bottlenecked by\ncranelift\" looks like from CI.\n\n**The counting trap.** `record_call` is called ONLY from `execute_function`, the\nper-iteration entry — not from compiled code, and not per inner call. So the\nonly function that accumulates a count is the entry (`main`), and it is counted\nabout `WARMUP_RUNS + BENCH_RUNS` = 25 times per benchmark. Any\n`blazing_threshold` above ~15 therefore never fires no matter how hot the\nprogram actually is. A first attempt at 50 was still dead for this reason.\n\nTwo consequences worth remembering:\n- Inner functions are never profiled at all; they arrive at a tier only because\n  a whole-module compile swept them up.\n- `should_promote` compares against the CURRENT tier, and the tiered backend\n  Cranelift-compiles every module at setup, so everything already sits at\n  Optimized. No `promote_target` event is ever emitted below Maximum — an empty\n  `RAYZOR_PROFILE_TIER_EVENTS=1` log means \"already there\", not \"not promoting\".\n\nOPEN, and NOT caused by the above: `rayzor-tiered` on binarytrees is ~13x\nslower than `rayzor-cranelift` (8459 vs 647 ms) at HEAD, with both at the\nCranelift tier. Profiling overhead is ruled out by the `record_call` finding.\nNext probe: compare against `rayzor-interpreter` — if they match, the entry is\nbeing interpreted rather than dispatched to its compiled pointer.\n\nSee [[feedback_perf_measurement_protocol]] and\n[[reference_nuc_build_incantation]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_benchmark_preset_never_reaches_llvm` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}